# Negative-control mutant — the consolidation submit REFUSES an invalid model
# before it writes (D68), exactly as the cancellation submit does.
#
# Naming: the sibling spec `contract-product.mutations.int.test.ts` already owns
#   `contract-product.mutations.consolidation-open-guard.must-fail.patch` (the
#   open gate), so this second concern for the same spec takes the
#   `<spec-basename>.<concern>` form, mirroring the cancellation pair
#   (`contract-product.mutations.cancellation-invalid-model.must-fail.patch`).
#
# Scenario it must flip: `submitConsolidation()` reads the open form's model and
#   resolves `false` when `invoiceConsolidationEnabled` is absent — a freshly
#   opened form whose empty model failed `checking` — so it SENDS NOTHING and
#   the validation errors from `checking` stay on context (D68). This mirrors
#   `submitCancellation()`, which resolves `false` on a model with no `option`.
# Test that goes red: contract-product.mutations.int.test.ts >
#   the AC-9 consolidation submit driven with an empty (invalid) model:
#   `submitConsolidation()` must resolve `false`, issue no
#   `PUT .../modify_invoice_consolidation`, and leave `validationErrors` set.
#   With the guard removed the submit sends `SET_CONSOLIDATION` unconditionally,
#   the machine's own `processing` re-validation lands in the form's `error`
#   region, and `resolveFormWrite` REJECTS — so the resolve-`false` assertion
#   flips and the "no request" assertion sees the write attempt.
# Mutation: drops the pre-send model guard (and its now-unused `isNil` import),
#   restoring the throw path the empty model took before D68.
#
# Apply with:  git apply <this file>   (run from repo root)
# Revert with: git apply -R <this file>
